      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 155,
      "outputTokens": 1172,
      "costUsd": 0.0106255,
      "pronunciationText": "Divide the rise in F by the width of the strip, and you get probability per unit of x, here per hour. Make the strip thinner and thinner, and that becomes the gradient of F. So we define the probability density function, f of x, as F prime of x, wherever F has a gradient. Here, f of x is two x over nine, between zero and three, and zero everywhere else. At one point five, F has gradient one third, so the density there is one third per hour. A density isn't a probability. It's probability per unit of x, like a rope's density in grams per metre. A high density means probability is packed in near that value, not that the exact value is likely."
    },
