      "inputTokens": 110,
      "outputTokens": 774,
      "costUsd": 0.007021,
      "pronunciationText": "Here's why that matters. Go back to the reaction-time histogram from last video. Divide each relative frequency by the class width, one tenth of a second. The heights become one, two point five, three point five, two and one, per second. Now each bar's area, height times width, is its probability, and the areas add to one. Three point five is bigger than one, and that's fine. A density can be bigger than one, because the probability is the area, not the height."
